{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "33",
    "case_summary": "A 25-year-old male presented to the emergency room with quadriparesis following a bicycle accident. He had a history of facial deformities since early childhood, which included nasal blockage, mouth breathing, mild snoring, and progressive bowing of the shins. There was no family history of similar conditions. The patient was employed and had been commuting by bicycle when he fell, landing on his face and sustaining facial bruising. He experienced immediate neck pain and weakness in all four limbs, with limited ability to lift his arms and move his legs. He was transferred to the hospital three days post-injury. Physical examination revealed symmetrical frontal and maxillary bossing, a symmetric soft tissue bulge of the hard palate, broad forearms, sausage-like fingers, and bowing of both legs with saber shins. The nasal bridge was flat and elevated due to soft tissue thickening. No café au lait spots were noted. Neurological examination showed grade 3 muscle power in the arms with significant distal hand muscle weakness and grade 2 muscle power in the legs. All deep tendon reflexes were absent, plantar reflexes were extensor, and there was a sensory level to all modalities at the groin.\n\nMRI of the spine showed diffuse enlargement of the laminae, transverse processes and spinous processes of the entire spine (mainly involving the posterior elements) with cervical canal stenosis and a cervical cord contusion at C 4–5 level. On T1 weighted images the posterior elements were hyperintense. The facet joints of the cervical vertebrae were also enlarged. Skull X-rays and CT scans with bone windows showed diffuse thickening of the inner and outer tables of all the skull bones with a widened diploe. The brain parenchyma, subarachnoid cisterns and paranasal sinuses were normal.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
